const title = "Bosch Dishwasher E15 Error: Meaning + Fix — Same Day";
const description = "Bosch dishwasher showing E15? It's the AquaStop anti-flood sensor reacting to water in the base. What triggers it, the safe reset, and finding the real leak.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What does E15 mean on a Bosch dishwasher?",
    a: "E15 is Bosch's anti-flood warning. A float switch in the base pan under the dishwasher has detected water where there shouldn't be any, and the AquaStop safety system has shut off the water and stopped the cycle to prevent a kitchen flood. The code is not the problem itself; it is the machine reacting to a small leak that let water collect in the base. Usually there is no dramatic puddle on the floor, just enough water underneath to lift the float."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I reset a Bosch E15 error?",
    a: "The safe reset is to get the water out of the base tray so the float drops. Turn off the dishwasher and unplug it (or switch off its breaker), pull it out from the cabinet if you can, and carefully tip it back about 45 degrees so the trapped water drains out of the base onto a towel. Let it dry, stand it back up, and restore power. E15 should clear. Important: this only resets the code. If a leak is still feeding water into the base, E15 will come back, and that means the leak needs to be found and fixed."
  },
  {
    q: "Why does my Bosch keep showing E15 after I reset it?",
    a: "Because the reset only empties the base; it doesn't stop what filled it. A returning E15 means there is an active leak somewhere, most often a worn door gasket, a failing water inlet valve, a cracked spray arm or tub seal, or a loose hose connection. Each drips into the base until the float trips again. Finding which one is the leak takes a proper inspection, and that is the point to bring in a tech rather than keep resetting."
  },
  {
    q: "Is E15 the same on a Thermador dishwasher?",
    a: "Yes. Thermador dishwashers are built on the same platform as Bosch and use the same AquaStop base-pan protection and the same E15 behavior. The meaning, the safe reset, and the leak-hunting approach are identical. Our techs service both."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I keep using the dishwasher with an E15 code?",
    a: "No, and you shouldn't want to. E15 exists specifically to stop a leak from becoming a flood, so the machine will refuse to run until the base is dry and the float resets. Even if you clear it, running it with an unfixed leak just re-trips the code and risks water damage. Get the leak found. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair."
  }
];

