<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">E15 is an anti-flood warning, and it means water reached the base.</h2>

    <p><strong>E15 on a Bosch dishwasher is the AquaStop safety system reacting to water in the base pan under the machine. A float switch down there has lifted, so the dishwasher shuts off the water and stops to prevent a flood. The safe reset is to drain that trapped water out of the base so the float drops, and the code clears. But E15 is a symptom of a small leak, so if the leak is still there, the code comes right back. The real repair is finding and fixing what's dripping into the base. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>Most people who get E15 don't see any water on the kitchen floor, which is exactly why the code confuses them. The leak is usually slow, and all of it collects in the hidden base tray rather than escaping onto the floor, until there's enough to trip the float. That is the AquaStop system doing its job. Our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair treat E15 as a leak hunt, not a code to keep clearing.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 WHAT TRIGGERS IT -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · What actually triggers E15</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A slow leak into the base pan.</h2>

    <p>The float in the base only lifts when water gets down there, so E15 always points back to a leak somewhere above it. The usual sources, roughly in order of how often we find them:</p>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Worn door gasket</h3>
      <p>The seal around the door hardens and cracks with age, letting a thin stream escape down the front and into the base during the cycle.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Failing water inlet valve</h3>
      <p>A valve that weeps even slightly sends water past its fitting and down into the tray over time.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Cracked spray arm or tub seal</h3>
      <p>A hairline crack or a tired seal releases water under pressure mid-cycle, which finds its way to the base.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Loose or split hose connection</h3>
      <p>The fill or drain hose connections can loosen or perish, dripping steadily into the pan until the float trips.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 SAFE RESET -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · The safe reset</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Drain the base tray to clear the code.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Cut the power.</strong> Turn the dishwasher off and unplug it, or switch off its breaker. Do not skip this.</li>
      <li><strong>Pull it out.</strong> Slide the dishwasher out of the cabinet far enough to move it, with a towel down.</li>
      <li><strong>Tip it back.</strong> Carefully tilt the machine backward about 45 degrees so the water trapped in the base drains out onto the towel. That is what drops the float.</li>
      <li><strong>Dry and restore.</strong> Let the base dry, stand the unit back up, and restore power. E15 should clear and the dishwasher will run again.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> draining the base and drying it is safe. Do not open the electrical panel, the pump, or the AquaStop valve assembly yourself. And remember the reset is temporary: if E15 returns, there is an active leak, and chasing it means removing panels and pressure-testing components. That's the tech's job, not a DIY one.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 WHEN TO CALL -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">If E15 comes back, it's a leak that needs finding.</h2>

    <p>A single E15 that clears and never returns was probably a one-time splash. An E15 that comes back after a reset means water is still reaching the base, and the only real fix is to trace and repair the leak. Our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair pull the lower panel, run the machine, and watch where water escapes, then replace the gasket, valve, seal, or hose that's responsible. We carry the common Bosch and Thermador parts, so most E15 leak repairs are a single visit. We quote before any work. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong> Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/dishwasher-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, Orange, and Ventura in most cases.</p>

    <p>Related: E15 is a leak code — if your Bosch is instead holding water and showing E24 or E25, that's a drain problem, covered on <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/bosch-not-draining/">Bosch dishwasher not draining</a>. Our <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/error-codes/">dishwasher error codes</a> guide decodes the rest of Bosch's E-codes alongside LG, Samsung, and GE, <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/leaking-water/">dishwasher leaking water</a> covers leaks that reach the floor, and brand coverage is on <a href="/brands/bosch-dishwasher-repair/">Bosch dishwasher repair</a>. Full model coverage on the <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/">dishwasher repair</a> hub. Nearby: <a href="/beverly-hills/dishwasher-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, <a href="/santa-monica/dishwasher-repair/">Santa Monica</a>. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

